,17767,17,22,481,220,21,66,62,17,488,272,62,16,284,220,16,59,1326,18,13,1124,4080,17,22,488,220,24,66,62,17,481,220,18,66,62,16,488,272,62,15,284,481,20,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThere exist constants $c_2,$ $c_1,$ and $c_0$ such that\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0.\\]Find $c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the constants \\(c_2\\), \\(c_1\\), and \\(c_0\\) in the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then compare coefficients with the left-hand side.\n\nFirst, expand \\((x-3)^3\\):\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27.\n\\]\nNow, let's expand the full expression on the right-hand side:\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 + c_2 (x-3)^2 + c_1 (x-3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 (x^2 - 6x + 9) + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0.\n\\]\nSimplify the expression by distributing and combining like terms:\n\\[\nx^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2 + c_1 x - 3c_1 + c_0 = x^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\n\\]\nWe now compare the coefficients of the expanded form with the original polynomial \\(x^3 + x^2 - 5\\):\n\\[\nx^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0) = x^3 + x^2 - 5.\n\\]\nBy comparing the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n1. \\(c_2 - 9 = 1\\),\n2. \\(27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1\\),\n3. \\(-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4eecb13e8494563460f7cf204620970d0fffb3e2d2a96835b8485dfb756495fd"},"seconds":24.00593066215515,"seed":595316365747877192,"stop":"eos","text":"From the first equation, we solve for \\(c_2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation to solve for \\(c_1\\):\n\\[\n27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\nFinally, substitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation to solve for \\(c_0\\):\n\\[\n-27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). We need to find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{2412}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2412}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"From the first equation, we solve for \\(c_2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation to solve for \\(c_1\\):\n\\[\n27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\nFinally, substitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation to solve for \\(c_0\\):\n\\[\n-27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). We need to find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{2412}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:417"}
